
{"id":"786b4897-e6ab-4c3b-bc6f-bf1c3dc61e02","object":"chat.completion","created":1784986143,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols', [])\\n    if not nodes or not isinstance(nodes, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Missing or invalid nodes')\\n    if not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError('required_symbols must be a list')\\n    required_set = set(required_symbols)\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node.get('id')\\n        if not nid:\\n            raise ValueError('Node missing id')\\n        if nid in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Duplicate node id: {nid}')\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    # validate parents exist\\n    for nid, node in node_map.items():\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None and parent not in node_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Parent {parent} not found for node {nid}')\\n    # sort nodes by depth descending, then by id string\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    present = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in present:\\n            continue\\n        # never remove root\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        # never remove a required symbol node\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_set:\\n            continue\\n        # only consider removable nodes\\n        if not node.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        # never remove a node with retained children (i.e., any child still present)\\n        child_ids = children_map.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in present for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        # tentatively remove: check all required symbols still present\\n        # need to simulate removal by checking symbols in present minus this node\\n        # But the node's own symbol is not required (we already skipped). However,\\n        # there could be required symbols only in its subtree (already removed children).\\n        # Since children are already removed (if any), we just need to ensure this node's\\n        # symbol is not the last occurrence of a required symbol. Since symbol not required, it's safe.\\n        # However, to be correct we compute presence:\\n        symbols_present = set()\\n        for id_ in present:\\n            if id_ == nid:\\n                continue\\n            symbols_present.add(node_map[id_]['symbol'])\\n        if not required_set.issubset(symbols_present):\\n            continue\\n        # remove node\\n        present.remove(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(present)\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce Python source code for solve(case). The case payload has nodes (list of dicts with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols list. We need to implement a reduction algorithm (Perses-inspired grammar-guided node reduction) that tries to remove removable nodes (tentatively) in a bottom-up order (deeper first), but preserve required symbols and the root. Also, we must not remove nodes with retained children (meaning if we remove a node, its children would be reparented? Actually typical Perses reduction: you can delete a node if its children can be promoted, or you can replace a node with its children? The description: \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" The oracle is interestingness_preserved, but in this task we only have required_symbols? There's no mention of an interestingness function; we need to preserve that all required symbols still appear in the tree after reduction? The fixture payloads have required_symbols, and we must never remove a node whose symbol is required, but also ensure that required symbols remain represented. Possibly we need to ensure that after removal, the required symbols still appear somewhere in the remaining tree. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" And \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That likely means we check if removing that node (and promoting its children) still keeps at least one node for each required symbol? Or does it mean the symbol of the node is not required, but removal might remove the last occurrence of a required symbol? The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node if its symbol is in required_symbols. Also, a node with retained children: if a node has children that are not also removed, we cannot remove it? Probably in reduction, you either delete a node and its subtree, or replace a node with its children (so children are retained). The phrase \"node with retained children\" suggests that if we plan to keep some children (i.e., they are not removed), then we cannot remove this node? Actually in Perses, you can delete a node if you also delete its children? But here it says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node if any of its children will remain in the final tree. That implies removal must be of a leaf node (no children) or we remove the entire subtree? But the description says \"deletion and replacement candidates\". Replacement: replace a node with its children (so remove node but retain children). That would be removing a node while retaining its children. But the constraint says \"never remove a node with retained children.\" That seems contradictory. Let's parse carefully: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" If we are doing a replacement (node -> its children), then we are removing the node but retaining its children. That would be a node with retained children. So that would be prohibited. Therefore maybe the intended interpretation is different: \"retained children\" means children that are themselves kept in the tree (i.e., not removed). In a replacement, the children are retained, so the node being removed has retained children -> prohibited. So perhaps replacement is not allowed? Only deletion of leaf nodes? But then why \"deletion and replacement candidates\"? Hmm.\n\nMaybe \"node with retained children\" refers to nodes that have children that are not candidates for removal? Actually, we are iterating over nodes in deeper-first order, and we tentatively remove a node. If we remove a node, we must also handle its children: either delete them or promote them. But if we promote children, they become children of the node's parent, so they are retained. That would be a \"node with retained children\" -> prohibited. So maybe we are only allowed to delete nodes that have no children (leaves) or delete a node and all its descendants? That would be deletion of a subtree. But then the requirement \"never remove ... a node with retained children\" would be satisfied if we delete the entire subtree (no children retained). However, the statement also says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" If we delete a subtree, we might lose required symbols if they are in that subtree. So we must check that after removal, all required symbols still appear elsewhere.\n\nBut the fixture payloads: nodes have \"removable\" boolean. So only removable nodes can be considered for removal. Also they have \"utility\" which may be used for ordering among equivalent-depth nodes? The description: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So for same depth, we sort by node ID? But also \"registered_candidate_order\" suggests we should consider a specific order? The description: \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" So registered_candidate_order might refer to the order in which nodes are presented? But the payload has nodes in some order. Maybe the reduction algorithm must consider nodes in the order they appear? But then \"stable_ties\" says use node ID for tie break. So likely we process nodes in depth order (deeper first), and within the same depth, by node ID (or the order in the list? The instruction: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" And \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID ascending? Node IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\". Lexicographic? Probably string comparison. That's fine.\n\nNow, what exactly is the reduction process? I think we need to implement a reduction that tries to remove removable nodes one by one, if it's safe (i.e., all required symbols remain present, and not root, not required symbol, not node with retained children). But after we remove a node, the tree changes: its children (if any) become children of its parent? Or if we delete a node, we also delete its children? The description says \"deletion and replacement candidates\". Replacement would be to replace a node with its children (so children remain). But per constraint, we cannot remove a node with retained children. So maybe we only allow deletion of leaf nodes (no children). But then what about internal nodes? They could be removed if we also delete all their descendants? But then children are not retained, they are deleted too. That would be a subtree deletion. But then the parent-child relationships: if we delete a node and its subtree, the node's parent loses that child entirely. That might be valid.\n\nBut the fixture data: nodes have parent references. The reduction likely simulates Perses: you consider a node and decide to delete it (remove entirely) or replace it with its children. The constraint \"never remove a node with retained children\" would disallow replacement. So only deletion of leaves or whole subtrees? Actually if you delete a leaf node, you are removing a node with no children -> allowed. If you delete an internal node and also delete all its descendants, you are not retaining any children -> allowed. But you must also ensure that ancestors are not removed while they still have other children that are retained? But the constraint is only about the node being removed, not its ancestors. So we can delete a subtree.\n\nGiven that, we need to design an algorithm that iterates over nodes in bottom-up order (deepest first), for each removable node that is not root, not required symbol, and not having children that will be retained (i.e., we plan to keep its children if we don't delete them), we check if removing it (and possibly its entire subtree) still leaves all required symbols present in the remaining tree. If yes, we mark it for removal, and then update the tree accordingly. However, the output required: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to output two things: retained IDs (list of node IDs that are kept after reduction), sorted (presumably alphabetically or by some order? \"sorted retained IDs\" suggests sort them, likely by ID string). And actual removal order: list of node IDs in the order they were removed. The removal order should be the sequence of decisions.\n\nAlso, we must consider that after removing a node, its children are removed as well (if we delete the subtree). So any node that is descendant of a removed node should not be considered for removal independently; they are already removed. So we need to keep track of which nodes are still present.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n- Parse case: get nodes list, required_symbols set.\n- Build mapping id->node dict, also build parent-child relationships: children of each parent.\n- Build depth ordering: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n- Maintain a set of present node IDs (initially all).\n- Maintain a list removal_order.\n- For each node in the sorted list (deepest first):\n   - If node id not in present set, skip (already removed).\n   - If node is root (parent null or id == \"root\"? root id is \"root\" in fixtures), skip.\n   - If node.symbol in required_symbols, skip.\n   - If node.removable is False, skip.\n   - Check if node has any children that are still present. If so, we need to decide: can we remove this node while keeping its children? That would be a replacement. But constraint says never remove a node with retained children. So if node has any present children, we cannot remove it. So skip. However, if we plan to also remove all its children, then the node would not have retained children. But the algorithm is processing bottom-up, so we might remove children first. If we remove all children, then when we get to the node, it will have no present children, so we can remove it. That would effectively delete the entire subtree. That is valid. So we process deepest first, so children are considered before parent. If children are removable and safe, they get removed, then parent can be removed later because it no longer has present children. That fits.\n   - If node has no present children (either originally leaf or all descendants removed), then we can consider removing it. Check if removing this node (i.e., deleting it and its entire subtree) would cause any required_symbol to be missing from the remaining tree. Since we already removed children, the only symbol contributed by this node is its own symbol. But note that if the node's symbol is required, we already skipped. So the node's own symbol is not required. However, there might be required symbols in the subtree that are already removed? But those required symbols would have been preserved because we would not have removed a node whose subtree contained the only occurrence of a required symbol. So we need to check globally: after hypothetical removal, are all required symbols still present somewhere? We can compute the set of symbols present in the current present set (excluding node and its descendants). Since we already removed children, the subtree is already gone. So present symbols include those from remaining nodes. But we must ensure that node's own symbol is not the last occurrence of any required symbol? Actually node's symbol is not required, so fine. But what about required symbols that were in descendants that were removed? That would have been checked when removing those descendants. So now, removing the parent node itself does not remove any additional symbols beyond its own symbol (which is not required). However, if the parent's symbol is not required, removal is safe. But we also need to consider that the parent may have multiple children that were removed, but they are already gone. So it seems the only check needed is whether the node itself is required; if not, removal is fine. But the instruction says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests we need to check that after removal, all required symbols still appear in the tree. Since we are removing a node that is not required and its subtree is already removed (or it has no children), the only potential loss is if the node itself was the only occurrence of some required symbol. But that can't happen because we skip required symbols. So maybe the check is unnecessary? But to be safe, we should compute the set of symbols present in the tree after removal (excluding node and its descendants) and compare to required_symbols. Since node's descendants are already not present (either removed or never existed), the effect is just removing node's own symbol. So we need to check if required_symbols are all still present in the remaining nodes. Since we maintain present set, we can simply check that after removing node, the set of symbols from present nodes (excluding node's id) still covers all required_symbols. That will pass because node's symbol is not required. So it's always true. However, there might be case where a required symbol is only present on a node that is a descendant of this node, but that descendant would have been removed earlier only if it was itself removable and safe. But that descendant might be required itself, so it would not have been removed. So if a required symbol is on a descendant, that descendant is still present. But if we remove the parent, the descendant becomes orphan? In a tree, if we remove a node, its children are also removed (if we do subtree deletion). But if the children are required and we removed them? That would be illegal. So we cannot remove a node if it has a child that is still present and is required. But we already skip nodes with present children. So that's covered.\n\nThus the algorithm is essentially: process nodes bottom-up; if node is removable, not root, not required symbol, and has no remaining children (i.e., leaves or nodes whose entire subtree has been removed), then mark it for removal. This matches Perses reduction where you delete nodes that have no children (or all children already deleted). But then the output should include retained IDs (nodes not removed) and removal order. The removal order will be the sequence in which we remove nodes, which should be bottom-up, deepest first.\n\nBut is that too trivial? The fixture payloads have nodes with \"removable\" flags, and we just remove all removable nodes that are not required and have no children left? But then some removable nodes may have children that are also removable, so they get removed first, then parent becomes removable. That seems plausible.\n\nBut we also need to consider \"replacement\" candidates. The description says \"deletion and replacement candidates\". Maybe there is also the option to replace a node with its children, i.e., remove the node but keep its children (reparent them to the node's parent). That would be a replacement. The constraint \"never remove a node with retained children\" would make that illegal. So maybe the interpretation is different: \"a node with retained children\" means a node that has children that are not removed (i.e., retained in the final tree). In a replacement, the children are retained. So that would be prohibited. Therefore only deletion of leaves or subtrees where all children are also deleted is allowed. So our algorithm stands.\n\nBut let's look at the fixture: in the first case, nodes: n1 (depth1, parent root, symbol s1, removable false, utility 0.339), n2 (depth1, removable true, symbol s2), n3 (depth1, removable false, symbol s0), n4 (depth2, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1), n5 (depth2, parent n3, removable false, symbol s2), n6 (depth2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0). required_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"].\n\nProcessing deepest first: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. n4: parent n2, removable true, symbol s1 (required!) -> skip because required. n5: parent n3, removable false -> skip. n6: parent n1, removable true, symbol s0 (not required), has children? none (depth2 leaf). So eligible for removal. Check: if remove n6, will required symbols remain? s1 appears on n1 (required symbol) and n4? Actually n4 is still present (required, not removed). So yes, s1 still present. So remove n6. Add to removal_order. Update present set: remove n6. Then next depth1 nodes: root, n1, n2, n3. n1: removable false, skip. n2: removable true, symbol s2 (not required), check children: originally had child n4 (depth2, symbol s1). n4 is still present (required, not removed). So n2 has present child -> skip. n3: removable false, skip. So no further removals. Retained IDs: all except n6. Sorted retained IDs: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? root is \"root\". Actually root id is \"root\". Sorted list: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. Removal order: [\"n6\"].\n\nBut wait, n4 is removable and has symbol s1 which is required; we never remove it because it's required. So that's fine.\n\nSecond case similar but different utilities. Third case similar. So the algorithm seems to produce a deterministic result based on removal of leaves that are removable and not required.\n\nBut is there any scenario where we should remove a node that has children but we also remove all children? That's covered because children removed first then parent becomes leaf and then removed. In the fixtures, n2 has child n4 which is required, so n2 cannot be removed. n1 has child n6 which is removable, n6 gets removed, then n1 becomes leaf but n1 is not removable (removable false). So n1 stays.\n\nThus the output for each case would be similar: remove the leaf that is removable and not required (n6), retain all others.\n\nBut we must also consider that the problem might expect us to also consider replacement? The description mentions \"deletion and replacement candidates\". Maybe replacement is allowed under certain conditions? The constraint \"never remove a node with retained children\" might be interpreted differently: \"retained children\" means children that are kept as part of the final tree after reduction? In a replacement, children are retained, so that would be prohibited. So replacement is not allowed. So only deletion.\n\nBut maybe the phrase \"node with retained children\" means a node that has children that are also candidates for removal? That doesn't make sense.\n\nLet's re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" This could mean: when considering removal, never remove a node that has children that are going to be retained (i.e., not removed). So if you want to remove a node, you must also remove all its children. That is consistent with subtree deletion. So we can remove nodes only if we also delete all their descendants. But to do that, we must ensure that those descendants are also removable (or we could force remove them? But they might be required). So effectively, we can remove a node only if it has no children (leaf) or all its children are already removed (so it becomes leaf). That's exactly our algorithm.\n\nThus the implementation is straightforward.\n\nBut we also need to return JSON with \"retained_ids\" sorted and \"removal_order\". Also might need to include \"reduced_tree\" or something? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So those are the required output fields. The output must be a JSON-serializable object, presumably a dict with those keys.\n\nFurthermore, the instructions say \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields. Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So if something is invalid (e.g., missing fields, invalid parent references), should raise ValueError.\n\nWe should validate: nodes list present, each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable (bool), utility (float). Required_symbols list present. Also ensure no cycles? Not necessary.\n\nEdge cases: if a node's parent doesn't exist, raise ValueError. Also ensure that root has parent null or None.\n\nNow, we need to produce Python source code. The function solve(case) receives the payload dict directly. It should return a dict with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". retained_ids: sorted list of node ids that are retained. removal_order: list of node ids in order of removal.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Build adjacency list children for each parent.\n- Build set of present ids.\n- Build list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id (string).\n- Iterate through that list, for each node:\n   - If id not in present: continue.\n   - Check root condition: if parent is None or id == \"root\" (but we can check if parent is None, but in data parent is null encoded as None in JSON? It's \"parent\": null, so in Python it's None). So skip if parent is None.\n   - Check required: if symbol in required_symbols: skip.\n   - Check removable: if not node['removable']: skip.\n   - Check children present: if any child id in present: skip.\n   - If we pass, then remove node: remove id from present, append to removal_order.\n- After loop, retained_ids = sorted(present).\n- Return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}.\n\nBut we must also consider that after removing a node, its parent might now have fewer children, but we already visited parent because we process deepest first. However, if we remove a leaf, its parent's child count decreases, but the parent might have been visited already (since depth shallower). That's fine because we only skip parents that have present children at the time of processing. If we remove all children of a parent before processing the parent, then when we get to the parent, it will have no children and may be removable. But we go depth descending, so parent (depth d) is processed after all children (depth d+1) because deeper first. So when processing parent, children already removed. So that works.\n\nBut careful: sorting by depth descending ensures that within same depth, order is by id. That should be fine.\n\nOne nuance: The node list may not be in any particular order. We should build the children list before sorting.\n\nNow, we need to output the source code as a JSON string. The entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\", whose value is a string containing the Python source. Must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWe'll write the Python code with proper indentation.\n\nLet's produce the code.\n\nAlso include validation: if nodes is empty or missing, raise ValueError. Also check that all parent references exist (except root). Also check that required_symbols is a list.\n\nWe'll implement as described.\n\nWe must not mutate the input case; we'll create a copy of nodes list? We only read from it.\n\nNow, produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1922,"completion_tokens":5569,"total_tokens":7491,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1920},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4858},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1920,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":2},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}